    {
      "id": "whQWg8fHPjDHYCQXhuRJj",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 188.83937151869978,
      "y": -1199.8757213827703,
      "width": 1169.9130119238484,
      "height": 778.066939815955,
      "angle": 0.008034356258475484,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a9",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1390937818,
      "version": 2843,
      "versionNonce": 1404842010,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781418116800,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "***application layer -> how our broswers and apps , translate the incoming bytes\n    **basically how the incoming bytes should be interpreted by our application \n    multiple protocols exists , here are some common ones\n    1. websockets -> bidirectional , real time , open ,fixed frames , understanding of when the message was   ended\nand when it started , xor with a key to avoid any cache poisioning(any intermideate server caching it , thinking of\nit as a http request)\n    url shifts from http to ws or wss if the ssl is there \n    [Header][Payload Length][Payload] s\n    2. http -> request response in various format , rest uses this \n    3. webRtc -> real time bi directional peer to peer \n\n***transport layer -> how the data is sent from port A to port B\n    note ** -> same port can be on multiple machines , how do you route to the correct machine ?? it is handled by\nthe internet layer , so \n   1. tcp -> http 1.1 , http 2 , websockets \n    realiable , confirm delivery , retries , network overhead of throttling and congetion control\n   as the inntermediate router have immediate meory , if you blas them with packets it will loose them due to space\nand then send the sack to get lost packet creating even more traffic , serialises the data , ack - sack stuff to\nestalbish connection\n\n   2. udp -> not reliable , port to port transmission , but very fast\n    used in webRtc , where some frames dropped is okay \n\n   3. QUIC -> based on udp , but applies , the tcp level features on top of it \n\n***. Internet layer -> finding the correct machine -> delhi -> router1 -> router2 ..\n\n      \n\n",
      "fontSize": 20.74845172842547,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "***application layer -> how our broswers and apps , translate the incoming bytes\n    **basically how the incoming bytes should be interpreted by our application \n    multiple protocols exists , here are some common ones\n    1. websockets -> bidirectional , real time , open ,fixed frames , understanding of when the message was   ended and when it started , xor with a key to avoid any cache poisioning(any intermideate server caching it , thinking of it as a http request)\n    url shifts from http to ws or wss if the ssl is there \n    [Header][Payload Length][Payload] s\n    2. http -> request response in various format , rest uses this \n    3. webRtc -> real time bi directional peer to peer \n\n***transport layer -> how the data is sent from port A to port B\n    note ** -> same port can be on multiple machines , how do you route to the correct machine ?? it is handled by the internet layer , so \n   1. tcp -> http 1.1 , http 2 , websockets \n    realiable , confirm delivery , retries , network overhead of throttling and congetion control\n   as the inntermediate router have immediate meory , if you blas them with packets it will loose them due to space and then send the sack to get lost packet creating even more traffic , serialises the data , ack - sack stuff to estalbish connection\n\n   2. udp -> not reliable , port to port transmission , but very fast\n    used in webRtc , where some frames dropped is okay \n\n   3. QUIC -> based on udp , but applies , the tcp level features on top of it \n\n***. Internet layer -> finding the correct machine -> delhi -> router1 -> router2 ..\n\n      \n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "8iNnR49QZ6rOouKdB1KYB",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 120.7501327505762,
      "y": -2767.261902559162,
      "width": 1470.8388671875,
      "height": 1350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aB",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1002217562,
      "version": 5629,
      "versionNonce": 237415002,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1781425636284,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "complete flow in terms of TCP/IP model \n*****NOTE -> every communication doesn't need all the layer\nfor eg -> for tcp handshake , we dont use fucking application layer\nCONTEXT -> we hit https://google.com ( if http no tls handshake)\n***** ssl certificates are the same as the tls \n\n1. DNS LOOKUP of the host \n--> we here as well use the same tcp/ip layer to achieve this ?\n   a. application layer -> os generates a dns query ( plain text ) ,\n   b. transport layer -> we use udp protocol here because we prioratise the speed\n        -> the os creates the udp datagram -> basically with udp header ( port addresses and stuff )\n            also has some metatdata like \n                              client port and destination port and stuff like that injected  \n        -> this udp datagram -> {header}{payload} -> payload is simply the dns query generated earlier\n   c. internet layer -> we need to convert it to the IPv4/IPv6 packet to sent it over the internet \n                           we encapusulate this udp data gram with the IP of the desitnation \n                       \n   d. network access -> we first again encapsulate with the mac address of the router or ithernet \n                              and send  it to the internet --> \n2.  ESTABLISHING THE CONNECTION WITH THE HOST\n    *** application layer is not used \n    a. transport layer -> we will use a tcp protocol at this step \n                              os will create a tcp Syn sgement -> basically the server will recognise this segment\n                              basically this segement will have bit high for the syn \n    b. internet layer -> os takes this syn segment and encapuslate this tcp syn segment with the servers ip address\n                             and gives the IPv4 packet \n    c. network access-> same steps --> leaves the device \n\n***there are further back and forth requests but we are good\n\n3. TLS handshake (securing keys and the certificates) ******only if the request was https \n    -> we have the connection established , lets share the keys , shall i really trust the server ?\n    -> we have the ip address with the exact port of the googles server  \n    a. application layer -> the browser creates a TLS client hello message (also includes the list of encryption it supports_\n    b. transport layer -> creates a tcp segment from the message \n    c. internet layer -> create ipv4 packet ......\n    d. network access -> ....\n\nmechanism of handshake \n** the google has certificate issued which is signed using a secret key only known to the issuing server \n** google send the our browser info about itself along with the certficate\n** the broswer has already the public keys , if we are able to verify the certificate signature with the public keys , it means the certificate is genuine\n    now , we see the certificate and it has the encryption method and the encrpted hash\n** browser hashes the content given by google using the same method and if the hash matches it means the google is claiming \n    exactly who he actually is \n    *** certificates cannot be forged by any one -- only using the secrety keys , but can be oppened by the public key only\n*** stuff needed to build the secret key is also shared to encrypt the next messages to be sent to avoid any sniffing 69\n\n4. HTTP REQUEST \n   -> we have the connection established and verified connection as well\n   a. application layer -> browswer generates the http request and encprytes it usinng the keys \n      note **> if we want websocket here , we add in the header upgrade -> websocket ... annd rest is handled \n   b. transport layer -> create tcp segments .... and everything okay \n    ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "complete flow in terms of TCP/IP model \n*****NOTE -> every communication doesn't need all the layer\nfor eg -> for tcp handshake , we dont use fucking application layer\nCONTEXT -> we hit https://google.com ( if http no tls handshake)\n***** ssl certificates are the same as the tls \n\n1. DNS LOOKUP of the host \n--> we here as well use the same tcp/ip layer to achieve this ?\n   a. application layer -> os generates a dns query ( plain text ) ,\n   b. transport layer -> we use udp protocol here because we prioratise the speed\n        -> the os creates the udp datagram -> basically with udp header ( port addresses and stuff )\n            also has some metatdata like \n                              client port and destination port and stuff like that injected  \n        -> this udp datagram -> {header}{payload} -> payload is simply the dns query generated earlier\n   c. internet layer -> we need to convert it to the IPv4/IPv6 packet to sent it over the internet \n                           we encapusulate this udp data gram with the IP of the desitnation \n                       \n   d. network access -> we first again encapsulate with the mac address of the router or ithernet \n                              and send  it to the internet --> \n2.  ESTABLISHING THE CONNECTION WITH THE HOST\n    *** application layer is not used \n    a. transport layer -> we will use a tcp protocol at this step \n                              os will create a tcp Syn sgement -> basically the server will recognise this segment\n                              basically this segement will have bit high for the syn \n    b. internet layer -> os takes this syn segment and encapuslate this tcp syn segment with the servers ip address\n                             and gives the IPv4 packet \n    c. network access-> same steps --> leaves the device \n\n***there are further back and forth requests but we are good\n\n3. TLS handshake (securing keys and the certificates) ******only if the request was https \n    -> we have the connection established , lets share the keys , shall i really trust the server ?\n    -> we have the ip address with the exact port of the googles server  \n    a. application layer -> the browser creates a TLS client hello message (also includes the list of encryption it supports_\n    b. transport layer -> creates a tcp segment from the message \n    c. internet layer -> create ipv4 packet ......\n    d. network access -> ....\n\nmechanism of handshake \n** the google has certificate issued which is signed using a secret key only known to the issuing server \n** google send the our browser info about itself along with the certficate\n** the broswer has already the public keys , if we are able to verify the certificate signature with the public keys , it means the certificate is genuine\n    now , we see the certificate and it has the encryption method and the encrpted hash\n** browser hashes the content given by google using the same method and if the hash matches it means the google is claiming \n    exactly who he actually is \n    *** certificates cannot be forged by any one -- only using the secrety keys , but can be oppened by the public key only\n*** stuff needed to build the secret key is also shared to encrypt the next messages to be sent to avoid any sniffing 69\n\n4. HTTP REQUEST \n   -> we have the connection established and verified connection as well\n   a. application layer -> browswer generates the http request and encprytes it usinng the keys \n      note **> if we want websocket here , we add in the header upgrade -> websocket ... annd rest is handled \n   b. transport layer -> create tcp segments .... and everything okay \n    ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
